/*! \page dapp_operations dApp Operator and Author Quickstart

For a single end-to-end walkthrough from a new out-of-tree repository through
catalog install, enable/disable, monitoring, and verification, start with
\ref dapp_quickstart. This page records operator policy detail: roles, signing,
declarative startup, Class-B evaluation, process hardening, Class-A
qualification commands, and the failure gallery.

\section dapp_roles Spectrum observer and authority roles

Class-C input subscription does not imply permission to affect the DU. A
spectrum package declaring neither
OCUDU_DAPP_SPECTRUM_CAP_INTERFERENCE_MAP_V1 nor
OCUDU_DAPP_SPECTRUM_CAP_QUIET_PERIOD_INTENT_V1 is an observer. It may consume
grids and maintain bounded private state, but it must not return either output.
The future-UL-plan capability requests an additional input route and is not, by
itself, result authority.

The DU permits one active spectrum result authority across native and
supervised-process placement. Capability-zero process observers may coexist
within the configured route, memory and process limits. ABI v1 retains one
native binding per interface, so process placement is the supported fanout
profile for multiple simultaneous observers. The SDK artifact
ref_spectrum_observerd_cpu is the executable reference.

An observer result is a protocol violation, not advisory output. The supervisor
withdraws every route before applying bounded restart policy; ordinary E3
metrics and incidents expose the event. Direct dApp-to-dApp calls are not an
alternative authority path. Cross-dApp composition uses host-owned typed
contexts and validates a single writer.

\section dapp_operator_flow Operator flow

At deployment time:

- Copy each immutable artifact and its generated manifest beneath the approved
  catalog root.
- Sign each exact manifest with \c ocudu-dapp-package, and configure a
  rollback-protected offline trust policy. Production defaults to rejecting
  unsigned packages.
- List normalized catalog-relative manifests in the DU configuration. Do not
  accept artifact paths, executable arguments or route descriptors over E3.
- Query inventory and verify package identity, interface, placement, backend
  and capability bits before loading.
- For each instance, run load, prepare, arm, shadow and activate in order. Wait
  for each asynchronous job and carry its returned instance generation into the
  next mutation.
- Query metric schema once, then monitor metric snapshots, health and incidents.
  Deactivate before unload; retry quiescence rather than forcing uncertain
  native code out of the address space.

For packages that must be present at every DU start, select a bounded initial
instance in the same operator YAML rather than scripting a race against radio
startup:

\code{.yaml}
dapp:
  package_manifests:
    - vendor-receiver.manifest.json
    - spectrum-observer.manifest.json
  initial_instances:
    - package_manifest: vendor-receiver.manifest.json
      placement: in_process
      backend: cuda
      target: shadow
      lifecycle_timeout_ms: 1000
    - package_manifest: spectrum-observer.manifest.json
      placement: out_of_process
      backend: cpu
      target: active
      lifecycle_timeout_ms: 2000
\endcode

Each entry must uniquely name one manifest already present in
\c package_manifests. At most 64 entries are accepted. Placement and backend
are checked against the signed manifest and host capabilities by the ordinary
load path; the timeout applies independently to prepare, arm, shadow, activate,
and any required cleanup transition. The only startup targets are \c shadow
and \c active. Use shadow when an operator must inspect inventory, stage a
model, or set module configuration through E3 before granting hook authority.

Initial startup is synchronous, all-or-nothing, and occurs before embedded or
local E3 ingress opens. A load, preflight, worker-readiness, lifecycle, target
state, or hook-authority failure unwinds every selected instance in reverse
through deactivate/quiesce/unload and fails DU service construction. Ordinary
service shutdown similarly retires startup-owned instances after management
ingress closes and after DU producers have stopped. Startup YAML cannot pass
opaque module arguments, artifact paths, or route descriptors and does not
define a second loader. Successfully started instances appear in normal E3
inventory and thereafter use the same lifecycle, configuration, model,
metrics, health, and incident operations as manually loaded instances. These
compatible fields remain part of working version v1.

The registered qualification campaign exercises this exact operator path with
both an unsigned development catalog and a required signed, SBOM-bound catalog.
It starts native spectrum active and a supervised SRS--ISAC worker in shadow,
changes the worker's v1 configuration, completes a v1 model
stage/validate/warm/activate sequence while publication remains zero, then
activates it and proves live publications before ordinary E3 unload. This is a
startup-policy test, not a second package or model version.

\subsection dapp_package_signing Offline package signing

Package authenticity is checked before native preflight, \c dlopen, or process
spawn. The detached \c MANIFEST.sig.json binds the exact manifest bytes and
a nonzero monotonic package sequence with ECDSA P-256/SHA-256. The manifest
continues to bind the immutable artifact SHA-256 independently. The operator
trust document names active public keys, revoked key IDs, a nonzero policy
epoch, and a minimum sequence for every admitted package ID. Revocation wins;
the configured minimum epoch and package floors reject rollback without a
network service or wall clock.

Create an offline file-backed operator key and sign a catalog manifest:

\code{.sh}
ocudu-dapp-package keygen \
  --private-key /secure/operator-dapp-key.pem \
  --public-key /secure/operator-dapp-key.pub.pem
ocudu-dapp-package sign \
  --catalog-root /opt/ocudu/dapps \
  --manifest vendor-receiver.manifest.json \
  --private-key /secure/operator-dapp-key.pem \
  --package-sequence 42
\endcode

Build a trust policy with every deployed package floor, then verify the exact
package before changing the DU configuration:

\code{.sh}
ocudu-dapp-package trust \
  --output /etc/ocudu/dapp-trust.json --policy-epoch 9 \
  --public-key /secure/operator-dapp-key.pub.pem \
  --package 01234567-89ab-cdef-0123-456789abcdef:42
ocudu-dapp-package inspect \
  --catalog-root /opt/ocudu/dapps \
  --manifest vendor-receiver.manifest.json \
  --trust-store /etc/ocudu/dapp-trust.json \
  --minimum-policy-epoch 9
\endcode

Configure \c package_signature_mode: required, the absolute
\c package_trust_store path, and the same or higher
\c minimum_trust_policy_epoch. Key rotation is an overlapping-key operation:
publish a higher-epoch trust policy containing old and new keys, re-sign at a
higher package sequence, then remove or revoke the old key in another
higher-epoch policy. Trust files and signature sidecars are bounded strict JSON;
their formal schemas are installed with the dApp SDK.

\c allow_unsigned is only an explicit local-development escape hatch. It admits
a genuinely absent sidecar, but a present malformed, unknown-key, revoked,
stale, or invalid signature still fails closed. Do not provision production
systems with this mode. E3 inventory reports only non-secret verification
status, signing-key ID, package sequence, and trust-policy epoch; it cannot
alter trust roots or unsigned policy. The v1 file-backed private key is
unencrypted and must remain offline with mode 0600; HSM integration is a future
operator-tool extension, not a DU runtime dependency.

The complete custody, immutable-generation, two-key rollover, revocation, and
installed-platform qualification procedure is defined in
\ref dapp_release_provisioning. All OCUDU-owned package and evidence contracts
remain v1 until a formal release decision; SPDX 2.3 is only the external SBOM
format identifier.

For a healthy process observer,
class_c.route.publications and class_c.copy.snapshot.count advance while
process.results_accepted, process.result_protocol_failures and steady-state
process.successful_restarts remain zero. Heartbeat responses must advance.
Activating a second authority fails with lifecycleFailed and a diagnostic that
another authority is active. Deactivate and unload the rejected error-state
instance, or first retire the current authority and retry with a fresh
transaction and generation.

Quiet-period intents are requests to host scheduling authority, not grants. The
operator bounds retained PRB-slot volume, PRBs suppressed in any one slot, and
PRB-slot volume in every rolling slot window with
quiet_max_retained_prb_slots, quiet_max_prbs_per_slot,
quiet_duty_window_slots and quiet_max_prb_slots_per_window. A conflict or duty
denial returns bypass to the originating dApp, preserves conventional
scheduling, and records a policyRejection incident with source instance,
generation, cell and invocation context. Such a denial is expected policy
behavior and does not open the dApp circuit breaker. Repeated equivalent
denials aggregate in bounded incident history and remain queryable through E3.

\section dapp_class_b_evaluation Class-B evaluation mode

Set scheduler_evaluation_only before DU startup to evaluate an admitted
Class-B scheduler package without granting it scheduling authority. The
instance still follows the ordinary prepare, arm, shadow and activate
lifecycle. Once active, each scheduler lane first computes the conventional
policy, invokes the dApp with the real bounded input, and validates the complete
dApp output. A valid dApp decision is then discarded and the conventional
decision remains authoritative. Invalid output is reported through the normal
incident and breaker path even in evaluation mode; suppressing authority does
not make malformed native output safe.

Evaluation mode is not the lifecycle SHADOW state. SHADOW prepares and warms a
native instance without binding it to radio hooks, so it cannot provide
representative callback timing or decision-quality evidence. Evaluation mode
is an ACTIVE, bound operating mode that provides such evidence while preserving
the conventional result. It is configured locally rather than mutated over E3
so that every scheduler lane has one immutable policy mode during operation.

E3 metric schema and snapshot operations expose the following cumulative host
measurements for a Class-B scheduler instance:

- class_b.policy.invocations
- class_b.policy.conventional_time_ns
- class_b.policy.dapp_time_ns
- class_b.policy.validation_time_ns
- class_b.policy.accepted
- class_b.policy.evaluation_accepted
- class_b.policy.fallbacks
- class_b.policy.validation_rejections
- class_b.policy.deadline_misses

The host records these measurements after the guarded callback rather than
trusting module-supplied telemetry. Before enabling authoritative mode,
operators should measure a representative workload, require zero validation
rejections, investigate every fallback and deadline miss, and compare dApp
latency and radio outcomes against a deployment-specific acceptance policy.
ABI v1 provides the evidence and static startup choice; dynamic promotion and
automatic KPI-based promotion are intentionally deferred until their safety
policy is specified and validated.

\section dapp_process_hardening Supervised-process hardening

Every admitted Class-C worker must set Linux no_new_privs before the host sends
any shared-memory route descriptor. A deployment may additionally require
Linux seccomp filter mode with class_c_process_require_seccomp_filter. The host
verifies both kernel states through procfs before transferring descriptors and
also requires the READY capability bit. A worker that does not satisfy the
selected profile is killed without receiving a route. Reference workers use a
TSYNC seccomp deny-list that blocks executable-image replacement, process
injection and privileged kernel administration while retaining the file/device
operations needed for CUDA initialization.

The supervisor installs irrevocable soft and hard RLIMIT_NOFILE bounds.
class_c_process_max_core_file_bytes defaults to zero, which disables worker CPU
cores. A nonzero field-debug value bounds RLIMIT_CORE; protect and prune the
kernel core destination because cores may contain tensor or model data. CUDA
workers receive only the allow-listed CUDA coredump environment when this limit
is nonzero. Operators may also set
class_c_process_max_address_space_bytes; zero inherits the deployment limit,
while a nonzero value must be at least 64 MiB and becomes RLIMIT_AS. The default
open-file ceiling is 512 and must accommodate the control endpoint plus three
descriptors per producer shard.

These controls reduce privilege-escalation and resource-exhaustion exposure;
they do not make arbitrary native code trustworthy. Package hashing,
capability checks, credential binding, bounded protocols, heartbeat recovery
and result validation remain required. Deployment-specific cgroups,
namespaces, distinct service identities, cgroups or Landlock may add stronger
containment, but are not core DU dependencies in ABI v1.

\section dapp_author_flow Author flow

Authors compile only against the installed OCUDUDAppSDK package. Use the
versioned C descriptor, one declared timing class, explicit placement/backend
masks, and only the capability bits actually implemented. Keep algorithm state
inside the opaque instance and per-worker contexts; do not use global mutable
state to bypass lane ownership. Return bounded status codes, never allow an
exception to cross the C ABI, and perform no E3, allocation, blocking I/O or
unbounded logging in a deadline-bearing callback.

For an observer process, consume and release every borrowed publication, answer
the authenticated control heartbeat, and emit no result record. The reference
worker and its manifest test demonstrate this contract. For an authority,
populate only the declared typed result, echo the admitted source identity, and
let the host stamp producer identity, validate time/generation, and commit it.

\section dapp_class_a_authoring Class-A CUDA authoring and qualification

Start a resident CE, CE+EQ or RX project with the standalone SDK repository's
\c tools/scaffold_class_a_cuda.py command. Select exactly one of \c ce,
\c equalizer or \c receiver and provide a new target, reverse-DNS global name,
package UUID and output directory. The generated project consumes only an
installed \c OCUDUDAppSDK package and contains only the selected algorithm
surface. It refuses to overwrite an existing directory.

Replace \c reference/HOOK/algorithm.cu, not the C ABI query trampoline or
lifecycle shell. Keep descriptor query pure. Allocate weights and immutable
instance state during prepare/model activation, and fixed lane scratch while
creating worker state. The invocation must use the caller's CUDA stream and
prepared output tensors; it must not allocate, synchronize, perform E3 work or
retain an invocation tensor. Keep the generated exception guard and, for RX,
the model-generation completion fence. A non-neural conventional algorithm is
a valid implementation of any of these hook contracts.

Equalizer (CE+EQ) outputs must include finite post-EQ noise for every admitted
data RE: after equalizer-dApp inline success the host derives CRC/scheduler
SINR from mean post-EQ noise (resident CE stub does not fill CSI). Host may
set optional \c dmrs.reserved[0] OFDM-symbol DMRS bitmask; packages may ignore
it and use coordinates. Soft host wall budgets default to 2000 us (demod) /
800 us (CE). See Markdown \c docs/dapp/quickstart.md and SDK
\c class_a_cuda_authoring.md.

Build the generated project against the installed SDK, then run the SDK's
\c certify_class_a_cuda against the final DSO and interface ID. The certificate
checks the declared minimum, representative and maximum shapes; one and two
concurrent streams; all four QAM orders; DM-RS Types 1 and 2; dense compact
outputs; caller-owned nonzero-offset storage and guards; lifecycle; metrics;
and optional receiver model generations. Keep its JSON result with the
separately hash-verified package manifest.

For host integration, build \c dapp_class_a_cuda_benchmark in a Release CUDA
configuration whose \c OCUDU_DAPP_REFERENCE_CUDA_DIR names the independently
built SDK artifacts. On an otherwise idle target GPU, run for example:

\code{.sh}
tests/benchmarks/dapp/dapp_class_a_cuda_benchmark \
  -R 1000 -W 100 -N 2 -P 25,106,273 -L 1,2 -M 2,4,6,8 \
  -C 100 -D 150 -G -O class-a-qualification.csv
\endcode

That command is one strict trial. Release admission uses the reproducible
protocol wrapper and an operator-owned policy copied from
\c tests/benchmarks/dapp/class_a_cuda_qualification_policy.example.json:

\code{.sh}
python3 tests/benchmarks/dapp/run_class_a_cuda_qualification.py \
  --benchmark build-release/tests/benchmarks/dapp/dapp_class_a_cuda_benchmark \
  --policy class-a-gb10-release-policy.json \
  --output-dir class-a-qualification-2026-07-13 \
  --trials 3 --lane-counts 1,2
\endcode

The output directory must not already exist. The runner fixes the v1 shape,
warm-up, sample, deadline, preflight and exclusive-GPU arguments rather than
forwarding arbitrary benchmark options. It runs at least three trials for the
one-lane baseline and each declared maximum-lane profile, continues after an
honest deadline failure, and independently recomputes matrix coverage,
admission, production misses and the P99.9 gate. Exit status zero means every
trial and policy check passed, two means valid evidence failed qualification,
and one means the evidence or execution was invalid. The final JSON hashes the
benchmark, every CSV and every log and uses
\c schemas/dapp/class-a-cuda-qualification-report-v1.schema.json. Preserve the
entire directory as one evidence unit.

The policy is not a performance waiver. It names the intended release platform
and exact scheduler, priority, CPU affinity and inherited PUSCH stream policy;
it also states the deployment GPU clock/power control and maximum temperature.
Optional fields constrain GPU identity, persistence/compute mode, minimum SM
clock and enforced power limit. The runner observes and validates what the
benchmark can measure but never changes scheduling, affinity, clocks or power.
An operator must replace every example value, arrange those controls before the
run, and explicitly attest that they are approved for release admission. A
false attestation value preserves useful evidence but makes the report fail.

\c -N selects the concurrent PUSCH-lane load; its default is one. Each lane is
one production-decorated demodulator with an immutable lane binding, inherited
CUDA stream and module worker context. One persistent host thread drives each
lane during a sampling phase. The benchmark never invokes one demodulator
object concurrently. It pools the per-lane samples and records the lane count
in every CSV row. Qualify both the one-lane baseline and the maximum simultaneous
PUSCH-lane profile admitted by the intended deployment; a one-lane pass cannot
qualify a multi-lane deployment.

\c -F selects packages whose global name contains the supplied substring and
records that filter in the CSV. It is intended for focused profiling (for
example, \c -F receiver); omit it for the complete qualification matrix. The
conventional comparison remains present in either mode, and a filter that
matches no registered package fails the run. \c -G refuses a package filter so
that qualification evidence always covers the complete package matrix.

Nsight instrumentation also enters the disposable native preflight helper and
can make its production 500-ms timeout expire before the measured path starts.
For a diagnostic-only profiler run, \c -T may raise that timeout up to 60~s and
records it in the CSV. \c -G refuses any value other than 500~ms; a relaxed
preflight run is never qualification evidence.

\c -G is the admission gate: it refuses unavailable/unhealthy NVML observation
or a pre-existing foreign compute client before constructing a PUSCH fixture,
and it fails if a client appears during the run, any resident path is bypassed,
the production completion deadline reports a miss, or device-pipeline P99.9
exceeds its budget. \c -X explicitly permits a monitored shared-GPU contention
run; such a result is diagnostic and must not be labeled exclusive
qualification. The CSV also records the OCUDU build, host, architecture, GPU
UUID/PCI identity, CUDA driver/runtime, NVML driver, CPU affinity, scheduler
policy/priority, requested PUSCH CUDA stream priority, and observed temperature,
SM/memory clocks, power, persistence, compute mode and performance-state
ranges. A value of -1 or \c unavailable means the platform did not expose that
telemetry and must never be presented as a controlled-value claim. The tool
observes but does not change those settings: run it with the same isolated CPU,
real-time scheduling and accelerator clock/power policy intended for the DU.
A failed tail gate is performance evidence, not permission to relax the budget
or relabel functional coverage as latency qualification. Run the focused
resident correctness/fallback tests and final DSO Compute Sanitizer
certificates in the same release before deployment.

\section dapp_failure_gallery Failure gallery

<table>
<tr><th>Failure</th><th>Host evidence</th><th>Required response</th></tr>
<tr><td>Second result authority</td><td>Activation job fails before publication enablement</td><td>Keep the current authority or deactivate/unload it before retry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Observer emits result</td><td>Protocol-failure metric and security incident; routes withdrawn</td><td>Disable/unload and correct the manifest or implementation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Worker misses heartbeat</td><td>Heartbeat-loss incident, exit/restart counters and new PID/generation</td><td>Inspect recurrence; unload if bounded recovery cannot stabilize it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ring full</td><td>Backpressure/drop metric; producer continues</td><td>Reduce rate or change measured bounded resources</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stale E3 generation</td><td>Conflict; no state change</td><td>Refresh inventory and retry with a new transaction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Callback throws or returns invalid output</td><td>Incident, breaker/fallback metric; conventional path retained where defined</td><td>Inspect incident and probe/reset only under policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quiet intent exceeds duty budget or conflicts</td><td>policyRejection incident; dApp remains active and conventional scheduling continues</td><td>Inspect the reason and tune the intent or operator duty envelope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Native quiescence uncertain</td><td>Resident quarantine</td><td>Retry quiescence or use a maintenance DU restart; never force unload</td></tr>
</table>

The live dummy-RU integration scenario runs one native authority and one real
process observer concurrently, rejects a second process authority, hands
authority to a process instance while the observer remains active, verifies
zero observer results/protocol failures/restarts, then exercises worker recovery
and clean unload through the shipped APER client.

The deterministic operator gallery is a separate registered campaign:

\code{.sh}
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure \
  -R '^dapp_dummy_ru_e2e\.operator_failure_gallery$'
\endcode

It uses four SDK artifacts that exist only when the standalone SDK is built
with \c BUILD_TESTING: native CE modules that throw, replace caller-owned
storage, or return a deadline status, and a process spectrum worker that emits
a source-invalid result. The campaign also creates an authority conflict,
stops a real worker long enough to miss its authenticated heartbeat, and kills
its restarted process. It requires conventional Class-A fallback, worker-local
breaker isolation, rejected process authority, bounded process recovery, E3
incident visibility, zero accepted invalid process results, terminal jobs, and
an empty live-instance inventory.

On success it atomically publishes
\c tests/integrationtests/dapp/dapp-failure-gallery-report.json beneath the
build tree. The report hash-binds the running DU and exact fault-package
manifests and conforms to
\c schemas/dapp/failure-gallery-report-v1.schema.json. Preserve that JSON as
functional containment evidence; it is not a latency or RF qualification
report.

The live CPU gallery's deadline module returns a synchronous deadline status,
so it proves fallback, breaker, metric, and incident behavior without claiming
to reproduce CUDA event timing. Accelerator completion that exceeds its
deadline cannot retroactively fall back the already returned invocation. Its
separate semantics and E3 observability are deterministically covered by
\c dapp_native_instance.async_completion_deadline_is_an_incident_but_not_a_current_call_fallback
and the PUSCH resident-adapter one-nanosecond completion-deadline fixtures.
Production CUDA qualification remains responsible for observing real event
completion under deployment load.
*/
